          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Theory: Property Relations, State Variables and Maxwell Relations",
          "content": "## Why this unit matters\n\nTemperature, pressure, composition, exact differentials, Maxwell relations and property pathways. The preceding prerequisite is **Material and Energy Balances, Book 2**; the destination is **Energy laws**. Engineering students should be able to move from a physical scenario to a transparent mathematical model and back to an interpretation of what would be observed in equipment. Thermodynamic design becomes unreliable when an input is precise to many decimal places while the phase model, standard states or material identities remain unspecified.\n\n## Four connected lessons\n\n### 1. Thermodynamic systems and state variables\n\nDistinguish closed and open boundaries; intensive and extensive properties; equilibrium state variables and composition.\n\n**Useful calculation:** U, H, G and A are state functions; specific and molar bases must not be mixed.\n\n**Physical interpretation:** A plant engineer chooses a control volume around the pump instead of the entire cooling loop. This choice determines which mass and energy transfers appear explicitly.\n\n**Failure to watch:** A laboratory sensor reads local temperature, while process calculations may assume uniform bulk temperature. Equilibrium should be justified before inserting the reading into a state equation.\n\n**Decision:** Translate tank, heat exchanger and nozzle sketches into declared thermodynamic boundaries.\n\n### 2. Exact differentials and state functions\n\nPath-independent property changes can be integrated along convenient reversible mathematical paths even when the real process is irreversible.\n\n**Useful calculation:** dU=T dS\u2212P dV for a simple compressible closed system of fixed composition.\n\n**Physical interpretation:** A gas is heated while expanded; the computed \u0394U is independent of whether the imaginary path is heating first or expanding first.\n\n**Failure to watch:** Heat and work depend on the real path and are not entries in a state-property table. Treating \u03b4Q as dQ can create impossible perpetual-energy predictions.\n\n**Decision:** Use consistency tests before combining measured thermodynamic data.\n\n### 3. Enthalpy, Helmholtz and Gibbs potentials\n\nLegendre transforms replace difficult natural variables with pressure or temperature, matching industrial measurements.\n\n**Useful calculation:** H=U+PV; A=U\u2212TS; G=H\u2212TS. For fixed composition dG=\u2212S dT+V dP.\n\n**Physical interpretation:** Flowing equipment is conveniently analyzed with enthalpy because flow work is incorporated in H.\n\n**Failure to watch:** Gibbs energy minimization applies at constant temperature and pressure for a closed reacting system, not every practical reactor without qualification.\n\n**Decision:** Map potential functions to compressor, batch reactor and flash-drum boundaries.\n\n### 4. Maxwell relations and measurable derivatives\n\nSecond mixed derivatives of thermodynamic potentials yield relations between difficult-to-measure entropy derivatives and accessible PVT derivatives.\n\n**Useful calculation:** (\u2202S/\u2202P)_T=\u2212(\u2202V/\u2202T)_P and (\u2202S/\u2202V)_T=(\u2202P/\u2202T)_V.\n\n**Physical interpretation:** An expansion coefficient measured at fixed pressure can be used to estimate entropy sensitivity to pressure for liquids.\n\n**Failure to watch:** Derivatives require the same phase, composition and smooth potential; near coexistence or critical conditions numeric finite differences become fragile.\n\n**Decision:** Design laboratory data checks that enforce derivative symmetry.\n\n## Unit assessment\n\n- **Problem 1A \u2014 Thermodynamic systems and state variables.** Given an engineering case of your choice, identify a defensible thermodynamic boundary and relevant equation, and justify its variables.\n- **Problem 1B \u2014 Data reliability.** Describe a measurement or property-data uncertainty that affects thermodynamic systems and state variables and a way to quantify its consequence.\n- **Problem 1C \u2014 Counterexample.** Construct an operating case where the assumption \u201cA laboratory sensor reads local temperature, while process calculations may assume uniform bulk temperature. Equilibrium should be justified before inserting the reading into a state equation.\u201d becomes important and would invalidate a simplistic answer.\n- **Problem 2A \u2014 Exact differentials and state functions.** Given an engineering case of your choice, identify a defensible thermodynamic boundary and relevant equation, and justify its variables.\n- **Problem 2B \u2014 Data reliability.** Describe a measurement or property-data uncertainty that affects exact differentials and state functions and a way to quantify its consequence.\n- **Problem 2C \u2014 Counterexample.** Construct an operating case where the assumption \u201cHeat and work depend on the real path and are not entries in a state-property table. Treating \u03b4Q as dQ can create impossible perpetual-energy predictions.\u201d becomes important and would invalidate a simplistic answer.\n- **Problem 3A \u2014 Enthalpy, Helmholtz and Gibbs potentials.** Given an engineering case of your choice, identify a defensible thermodynamic boundary and relevant equation, and justify its variables.\n- **Problem 3B \u2014 Data reliability.** Describe a measurement or property-data uncertainty that affects enthalpy, helmholtz and gibbs potentials and a way to quantify its consequence.\n- **Problem 3C \u2014 Counterexample.** Construct an operating case where the assumption \u201cGibbs energy minimization applies at constant temperature and pressure for a closed reacting system, not every practical reactor without qualification.\u201d becomes important and would invalidate a simplistic answer.\n- **Problem 4A \u2014 Maxwell relations and measurable derivatives.** Given an engineering case of your choice, identify a defensible thermodynamic boundary and relevant equation, and justify its variables.\n- **Problem 4B \u2014 Data reliability.** Describe a measurement or property-data uncertainty that affects maxwell relations and measurable derivatives and a way to quantify its consequence.\n- **Problem 4C \u2014 Counterexample.** Construct an operating case where the assumption \u201cDerivatives require the same phase, composition and smooth potential; near coexistence or critical conditions numeric finite differences become fragile.\u201d becomes important and would invalidate a simplistic answer.\n\n## Assessment evidence"
