          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 1: Thermodynamic systems and state variables",
          "content": "# Unit 1 \u00b7 Lesson 1: Thermodynamic systems and state variables\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain thermodynamic systems and state variables in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nDistinguish closed and open boundaries; intensive and extensive properties; equilibrium state variables and composition.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** U, H, G and A are state functions; specific and molar bases must not be mixed.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nA plant engineer chooses a control volume around the pump instead of the entire cooling loop. This choice determines which mass and energy transfers appear explicitly.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA storage vessel holds 2.4 kmol nitrogen and a measured 305 K bulk gas temperature. Before estimating pressure, decide whether the gas volume is fixed and whether a single homogeneous state is plausible.\n\nA measurement at the wall may differ from the core during rapid filling, so the correct first step is a sensor-location and transient review.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nA laboratory sensor reads local temperature, while process calculations may assume uniform bulk temperature. Equilibrium should be justified before inserting the reading into a state equation.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nTranslate tank, heat exchanger and nozzle sketches into declared thermodynamic boundaries.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for thermodynamic systems and state variables.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Thermodynamic systems and state variables** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between thermodynamic systems and state variables and the application \u201cTranslate tank, heat exchanger and nozzle sketches into declared thermodynamic boundaries.\u201d The governing relation is **U, H, G and A are state functions; specific and molar bases must not be mixed.**; the stated failure mode is **A laboratory sensor reads local temperature, while process calculations may assume uniform bulk temperature. Equilibrium should be justified before inserting the reading into a state equation.**"
