          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 2: Exact differentials and state functions",
          "content": "# Unit 1 \u00b7 Lesson 2: Exact differentials and state functions\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain exact differentials and state functions in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nPath-independent property changes can be integrated along convenient reversible mathematical paths even when the real process is irreversible.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** dU=T dS\u2212P dV for a simple compressible closed system of fixed composition.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nA gas is heated while expanded; the computed \u0394U is independent of whether the imaginary path is heating first or expanding first.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA 1.2 mol working fluid moves from state A to state B by either heating first or expanding first. Compare only the tabulated state-function difference, not the individual heat and work legs.\n\nThe two hypothetical reversible paths may yield different heat and work yet the same U(B)-U(A), exposing a common bookkeeping misconception.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nHeat and work depend on the real path and are not entries in a state-property table. Treating \u03b4Q as dQ can create impossible perpetual-energy predictions.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nUse consistency tests before combining measured thermodynamic data.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for exact differentials and state functions.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Exact differentials and state functions** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between exact differentials and state functions and the application \u201cUse consistency tests before combining measured thermodynamic data.\u201d The governing relation is **dU=T dS\u2212P dV for a simple compressible closed system of fixed composition.**; the stated failure mode is **Heat and work depend on the real path and are not entries in a state-property table. Treating \u03b4Q as dQ can create impossible perpetual-energy predictions.**"
