          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 3: Enthalpy, Helmholtz and Gibbs potentials",
          "content": "# Unit 1 \u00b7 Lesson 3: Enthalpy, Helmholtz and Gibbs potentials\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain enthalpy, helmholtz and gibbs potentials in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nLegendre transforms replace difficult natural variables with pressure or temperature, matching industrial measurements.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** H=U+PV; A=U\u2212TS; G=H\u2212TS. For fixed composition dG=\u2212S dT+V dP.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nFlowing equipment is conveniently analyzed with enthalpy because flow work is incorporated in H.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA steam line supplies a high-pressure exchanger at 550 K. Choose enthalpy for the steady control-volume balance and Gibbs energy for assessing chemical equilibrium only if reactions matter.\n\nSelecting a property potential by convenience does not repeal the underlying conservation law; a physically correct state model remains essential.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nGibbs energy minimization applies at constant temperature and pressure for a closed reacting system, not every practical reactor without qualification.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nMap potential functions to compressor, batch reactor and flash-drum boundaries.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for enthalpy, helmholtz and gibbs potentials.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Enthalpy, Helmholtz and Gibbs potentials** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between enthalpy, helmholtz and gibbs potentials and the application \u201cMap potential functions to compressor, batch reactor and flash-drum boundaries.\u201d The governing relation is **H=U+PV; A=U\u2212TS; G=H\u2212TS. For fixed composition dG=\u2212S dT+V dP.**; the stated failure mode is **Gibbs energy minimization applies at constant temperature and pressure for a closed reacting system, not every practical reactor without qualification.**"
