          "secNumber": "1.5",
          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 4: Maxwell relations and measurable derivatives",
          "content": "# Unit 1 \u00b7 Lesson 4: Maxwell relations and measurable derivatives\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain maxwell relations and measurable derivatives in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nSecond mixed derivatives of thermodynamic potentials yield relations between difficult-to-measure entropy derivatives and accessible PVT derivatives.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** (\u2202S/\u2202P)_T=\u2212(\u2202V/\u2202T)_P and (\u2202S/\u2202V)_T=(\u2202P/\u2202T)_V.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nAn expansion coefficient measured at fixed pressure can be used to estimate entropy sensitivity to pressure for liquids.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA liquid volume at constant pressure expands measurably between 295 and 310 K. Use the expansion coefficient to discuss the sign of (\u2202S/\u2202P)_T rather than guessing entropy pressure dependence.\n\nPositive thermal expansion implies a negative entropy-pressure derivative at constant temperature for a simple stable liquid.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nDerivatives require the same phase, composition and smooth potential; near coexistence or critical conditions numeric finite differences become fragile.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nDesign laboratory data checks that enforce derivative symmetry.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for maxwell relations and measurable derivatives.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Maxwell relations and measurable derivatives** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between maxwell relations and measurable derivatives and the application \u201cDesign laboratory data checks that enforce derivative symmetry.\u201d The governing relation is **(\u2202S/\u2202P)_T=\u2212(\u2202V/\u2202T)_P and (\u2202S/\u2202V)_T=(\u2202P/\u2202T)_V.**; the stated failure mode is **Derivatives require the same phase, composition and smooth potential; near coexistence or critical conditions numeric finite differences become fragile.**"
