          "secNumber": "1.8",
          "heading": "Experimental Laboratory Investigation: Laboratory 17: van\u2019t Hoff equilibrium constant",
          "content": "# Laboratory 17: van\u2019t Hoff equilibrium constant\n\n**Model:** K=Kref exp[\u2212\u0394H/R(1/T\u22121/Tref)]\n\n**Controllable variables:** Temperature (K) and Reaction enthalpy (kJ mol\u207b\u00b9).\n\n## Governing relation\n\nK=Kref exp[\u2212\u0394H/R(1/T\u22121/Tref)]. This relation is used for an educational screening calculation, not certified physical-property or process-safety design. Every output must be checked against dimensional consistency, equilibrium assumptions and an independent reference when applied to real chemicals.\n\n## Stated limitations\n\nReaction \u0394H constant; Kref=4 at 300 K; dimensionless standard state.\n\n## Step-by-step investigation\n\n1. Record the default inputs and the readout with its units. Sketch the qualitative expected trend before inspecting the chart.\n2. Decrease **Temperature** to its minimum while keeping **Reaction enthalpy** fixed. Record the change and explain which term in the governing equation is responsible.\n3. Increase **Temperature** to its maximum and compare the response with the first observation. Determine whether the dependence is linear, logarithmic, exponential or nonlinear for a reason you can explain.\n4. Reset, then alter **Reaction enthalpy** and repeat. Estimate a finite-difference sensitivity between two nearby parameter values, including its units.\n5. Export the response curve to CSV. Explain how a plotted sensitivity helps select a range for a laboratory measurement or a process-control test.\n6. Identify the first physical mechanism omitted by the model that would become important if the system were pressurized, heated, mixed or scaled up substantially.\n\n## Questions\n\n**Reasoning:** Why does exothermic equilibrium shift away from products at higher temperature?\n\n**Extension:** What changes if reaction enthalpy becomes temperature-dependent?\n\n**Uncertainty:** How would uncertainty in reaction enthalpy influence your interpretation of the response curve? Which single additional measurement would best distinguish model error from input uncertainty?\n\n## Evidence to submit\n\nExport a parameter sweep from the interactive model and attach it with your explanation of **K=Kref exp[\u2212\u0394H/R(1/T\u22121/Tref)]**. Demonstrate the predicted change in **Temperature** at two different settings of **Reaction enthalpy**. Conclude by explaining whether the laboratory's approximation **(Reaction \u0394H constant; Kref=4 at 300 K; dimensionless standard state.)** would remain credible in a real chemical process."
