          "secNumber": "2.2",
          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 1: First law for flowing equipment",
          "content": "# Unit 2 \u00b7 Lesson 1: First law for flowing equipment\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain first law for flowing equipment in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nSteady-flow energy balances include enthalpy, kinetic, potential, shaft work and heat transfer with a declared sign convention.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** For a one-inlet one-outlet steady device: q\u2212w_s = \u0394h+\u0394(v\u00b2/2)+g\u0394z.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nAn insulated turbine can convert a decrease in steam enthalpy into useful shaft work; a valve with negligible kinetic change has approximately equal inlet and outlet enthalpy.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA turbine receives 3 kg/s steam. Its measured specific-enthalpy drop is 120 kJ/kg with negligible heat loss and kinetic terms. The approximate shaft output is 360 kW.\n\nThe sign convention must be stated: work leaving the turbine is positive in this balance; unexplained temperature-based estimates are weaker than enthalpy data.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nNeglecting velocity and elevation must be justified; a nozzle is precisely the equipment where kinetic energy dominates.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nBuild balances for turbines, throttles, pumps and heat exchangers.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for first law for flowing equipment.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **First law for flowing equipment** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between first law for flowing equipment and the application \u201cBuild balances for turbines, throttles, pumps and heat exchangers.\u201d The governing relation is **For a one-inlet one-outlet steady device: q\u2212w_s = \u0394h+\u0394(v\u00b2/2)+g\u0394z.**; the stated failure mode is **Neglecting velocity and elevation must be justified; a nozzle is precisely the equipment where kinetic energy dominates.**"
