          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 2: Entropy balance and second-law limits",
          "content": "# Unit 2 \u00b7 Lesson 2: Entropy balance and second-law limits\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain entropy balance and second-law limits in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nEntropy transport by heat and mass is supplemented by nonnegative entropy generation in real processes.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** At steady state: 0=\u03a3 m_in s_in\u2212\u03a3 m_out s_out+\u03a3 Q_j/T_j+S_gen, with S_gen\u22650.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nHeat leaking through a finite temperature difference creates entropy even if energy is fully conserved.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA 12 kW heater transfers energy from a 430 K reservoir to water at 320 K. Compare energy conservation with entropy generated by the transfer.\n\nWith both temperatures held fixed, entropy generation from the reservoirs is Q(1/320-1/430); the process conserves energy but destroys available work.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nA negative computed S_gen in a passive device signals inconsistent signs, inaccurate state data or an invalid process assumption.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nEstimate irreversibility in mixing and heat exchange.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for entropy balance and second-law limits.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Entropy balance and second-law limits** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between entropy balance and second-law limits and the application \u201cEstimate irreversibility in mixing and heat exchange.\u201d The governing relation is **At steady state: 0=\u03a3 m_in s_in\u2212\u03a3 m_out s_out+\u03a3 Q_j/T_j+S_gen, with S_gen\u22650.**; the stated failure mode is **A negative computed S_gen in a passive device signals inconsistent signs, inaccurate state data or an invalid process assumption.**"
