          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 3: Exergy and lost-work accounting",
          "content": "# Unit 2 \u00b7 Lesson 3: Exergy and lost-work accounting\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain exergy and lost-work accounting in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nAvailable useful work depends on both the system and its chosen environmental reference state.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** Specific flow exergy \u03c8=(h\u2212h0)\u2212T0(s\u2212s0)+V\u00b2/2+gz; destroyed exergy=T0 S_gen.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nTwo streams having equal energy can possess very different work potential depending on their temperature and pressure relative to the environment.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA hot process stream and cooling water deliver the same energy as a high-pressure steam utility. Estimate their different maximum useful work at a common 298 K ambient state.\n\nEnergy is insufficient to rank resources: their ability to produce useful work depends on the thermodynamic distance to the environment.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nExergy is not a conserved property: it is destroyed by irreversibility, and its value depends on ambient temperature and composition assumptions.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nLocate avoidable losses in plant utility systems.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for exergy and lost-work accounting.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Exergy and lost-work accounting** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between exergy and lost-work accounting and the application \u201cLocate avoidable losses in plant utility systems.\u201d The governing relation is **Specific flow exergy \u03c8=(h\u2212h0)\u2212T0(s\u2212s0)+V\u00b2/2+gz; destroyed exergy=T0 S_gen.**; the stated failure mode is **Exergy is not a conserved property: it is destroyed by irreversibility, and its value depends on ambient temperature and composition assumptions.**"
