          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 4: Cycles and thermodynamic efficiency",
          "content": "# Unit 2 \u00b7 Lesson 4: Cycles and thermodynamic efficiency\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain cycles and thermodynamic efficiency in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nA cycle returns its working fluid to its initial state while transferring energy and entropy to external reservoirs.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** Carnot engine \u03b7_max=1\u2212T_c/T_h; ideal refrigerator COP_max=T_c/(T_h\u2212T_c), using kelvin.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nA heat pump should be compared using COP, not a thermal engine efficiency, because the useful output is heat delivered to a space.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nAn engine operates between reservoirs at 600 K and 300 K. Its reversible upper efficiency is 0.50, yet its measured efficiency is lower.\n\nA real engine cannot eliminate finite temperature differences, friction or pressure drops; the half-efficiency limit is a comparison, not a design promise.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nCarnot limits require heat exchange with thermal reservoirs and exclude real pressure drops, mixing and finite heat-transfer rates.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nCompare ideal cycles with feasible process utilities.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for cycles and thermodynamic efficiency.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Cycles and thermodynamic efficiency** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between cycles and thermodynamic efficiency and the application \u201cCompare ideal cycles with feasible process utilities.\u201d The governing relation is **Carnot engine \u03b7_max=1\u2212T_c/T_h; ideal refrigerator COP_max=T_c/(T_h\u2212T_c), using kelvin.**; the stated failure mode is **Carnot limits require heat exchange with thermal reservoirs and exclude real pressure drops, mixing and finite heat-transfer rates.**"
