          "content": "# Laboratory 18: Adiabatic compressor model\n\n**Model:** T2=T1+[T1(r^((k\u22121)/k)\u22121)]/\u03b7\n\n**Controllable variables:** Pressure ratio (ratio) and Isentropic efficiency (fraction).\n\n## Governing relation\n\nT2=T1+[T1(r^((k\u22121)/k)\u22121)]/\u03b7. This relation is used for an educational screening calculation, not certified physical-property or process-safety design. Every output must be checked against dimensional consistency, equilibrium assumptions and an independent reference when applied to real chemicals.\n\n## Stated limitations\n\nIdeal gas, k=1.4, T1=300 K, constant heat capacities, adiabatic compressor.\n\n## Step-by-step investigation\n\n1. Record the default inputs and the readout with its units. Sketch the qualitative expected trend before inspecting the chart.\n2. Decrease **Pressure ratio** to its minimum while keeping **Isentropic efficiency** fixed. Record the change and explain which term in the governing equation is responsible.\n3. Increase **Pressure ratio** to its maximum and compare the response with the first observation. Determine whether the dependence is linear, logarithmic, exponential or nonlinear for a reason you can explain.\n4. Reset, then alter **Isentropic efficiency** and repeat. Estimate a finite-difference sensitivity between two nearby parameter values, including its units.\n5. Export the response curve to CSV. Explain how a plotted sensitivity helps select a range for a laboratory measurement or a process-control test.\n6. Identify the first physical mechanism omitted by the model that would become important if the system were pressurized, heated, mixed or scaled up substantially.\n\n## Questions\n\n**Reasoning:** Why is actual outlet temperature greater than the isentropic benchmark?\n\n**Extension:** Increase \u03b7 and estimate the change in discharge temperature.\n\n**Uncertainty:** How would uncertainty in isentropic efficiency influence your interpretation of the response curve? Which single additional measurement would best distinguish model error from input uncertainty?\n\n## Evidence to submit\n\nExport a parameter sweep from the interactive model and attach it with your explanation of **T2=T1+[T1(r^((k\u22121)/k)\u22121)]/\u03b7**. Demonstrate the predicted change in **Pressure ratio** at two different settings of **Isentropic efficiency**. Conclude by explaining whether the laboratory's approximation **(Ideal gas, k=1.4, T1=300 K, constant heat capacities, adiabatic compressor.)** would remain credible in a real chemical process."
