          "secNumber": "3.5",
          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 4: Residual functions and property data",
          "content": "# Unit 3 \u00b7 Lesson 4: Residual functions and property data\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain residual functions and property data in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nA consistent equation of state can determine departures of enthalpy and entropy from ideal-gas reference values.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** h=h^ig+h^R; s=s^ig+s^R. Departure values depend on T,P and chosen EOS.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nA compressor calculation may use an ideal-gas thermal contribution plus a residual correction when actual state accuracy demands it.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA compressor duty is first computed from Cp \u0394T and then checked with a departure-property correlation. Compare predicted duties against uncertainty in Cp and pressure measurements.\n\nData reconciliation is valuable only if caloric and PVT calculations originate from a consistent reference state and EOS.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nDo not add unrelated property correlations that violate a Maxwell relation or imply an impossible negative compressibility.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nDesign a property-data workflow using documented references and uncertainty.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for residual functions and property data.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Residual functions and property data** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between residual functions and property data and the application \u201cDesign a property-data workflow using documented references and uncertainty.\u201d The governing relation is **h=h^ig+h^R; s=s^ig+s^R. Departure values depend on T,P and chosen EOS.**; the stated failure mode is **Do not add unrelated property correlations that violate a Maxwell relation or imply an impossible negative compressibility.**"
