          "secNumber": "3.6",
          "heading": "Experimental Laboratory Investigation: Laboratory 5: Availability of process heat",
          "content": "# Laboratory 5: Availability of process heat\n\n**Model:** Wmax=Q(1\u2212T0/T)\n\n**Controllable variables:** Source temperature (K) and Heat transfer rate (kW).\n\n## Governing relation\n\nWmax=Q(1\u2212T0/T). This relation is used for an educational screening calculation, not certified physical-property or process-safety design. Every output must be checked against dimensional consistency, equilibrium assumptions and an independent reference when applied to real chemicals.\n\n## Stated limitations\n\nReversible heat extraction from reservoir at T; environment 298.15 K.\n\n## Step-by-step investigation\n\n1. Record the default inputs and the readout with its units. Sketch the qualitative expected trend before inspecting the chart.\n2. Decrease **Source temperature** to its minimum while keeping **Heat transfer rate** fixed. Record the change and explain which term in the governing equation is responsible.\n3. Increase **Source temperature** to its maximum and compare the response with the first observation. Determine whether the dependence is linear, logarithmic, exponential or nonlinear for a reason you can explain.\n4. Reset, then alter **Heat transfer rate** and repeat. Estimate a finite-difference sensitivity between two nearby parameter values, including its units.\n5. Export the response curve to CSV. Explain how a plotted sensitivity helps select a range for a laboratory measurement or a process-control test.\n6. Identify the first physical mechanism omitted by the model that would become important if the system were pressurized, heated, mixed or scaled up substantially.\n\n## Questions\n\n**Reasoning:** Why is one kilowatt of low-grade heat less useful than high-grade heat?\n\n**Extension:** Compare source temperatures delivering equal thermal energy.\n\n**Uncertainty:** How would uncertainty in heat transfer rate influence your interpretation of the response curve? Which single additional measurement would best distinguish model error from input uncertainty?\n\n## Evidence to submit\n\nExport a parameter sweep from the interactive model and attach it with your explanation of **Wmax=Q(1\u2212T0/T)**. Demonstrate the predicted change in **Source temperature** at two different settings of **Heat transfer rate**. Conclude by explaining whether the laboratory's approximation **(Reversible heat extraction from reservoir at T; environment 298.15 K.)** would remain credible in a real chemical process."
