          "heading": "Experimental Laboratory Investigation: Laboratory 6: Van der Waals isotherms",
          "content": "# Laboratory 6: Van der Waals isotherms\n\n**Model:** P=RT/(v\u2212b)\u2212a/v\u00b2\n\n**Controllable variables:** Molar volume (L mol\u207b\u00b9) and Temperature (K).\n\n## Governing relation\n\nP=RT/(v\u2212b)\u2212a/v\u00b2. This relation is used for an educational screening calculation, not certified physical-property or process-safety design. Every output must be checked against dimensional consistency, equilibrium assumptions and an independent reference when applied to real chemicals.\n\n## Stated limitations\n\nIllustrative a=0.40 MPa L\u00b2/mol\u00b2 and b=0.04 L/mol; excludes coexistence construction.\n\n## Step-by-step investigation\n\n1. Record the default inputs and the readout with its units. Sketch the qualitative expected trend before inspecting the chart.\n2. Decrease **Molar volume** to its minimum while keeping **Temperature** fixed. Record the change and explain which term in the governing equation is responsible.\n3. Increase **Molar volume** to its maximum and compare the response with the first observation. Determine whether the dependence is linear, logarithmic, exponential or nonlinear for a reason you can explain.\n4. Reset, then alter **Temperature** and repeat. Estimate a finite-difference sensitivity between two nearby parameter values, including its units.\n5. Export the response curve to CSV. Explain how a plotted sensitivity helps select a range for a laboratory measurement or a process-control test.\n6. Identify the first physical mechanism omitted by the model that would become important if the system were pressurized, heated, mixed or scaled up substantially.\n\n## Questions\n\n**Reasoning:** Where do nonphysical isotherm loops appear in a cubic equation of state?\n\n**Extension:** Increase temperature and inspect how isotherm shape changes.\n\n**Uncertainty:** How would uncertainty in temperature influence your interpretation of the response curve? Which single additional measurement would best distinguish model error from input uncertainty?\n\n## Evidence to submit\n\nExport a parameter sweep from the interactive model and attach it with your explanation of **P=RT/(v\u2212b)\u2212a/v\u00b2**. Demonstrate the predicted change in **Molar volume** at two different settings of **Temperature**. Conclude by explaining whether the laboratory's approximation **(Illustrative a=0.40 MPa L\u00b2/mol\u00b2 and b=0.04 L/mol; excludes coexistence construction.)** would remain credible in a real chemical process."
