          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 2: Chemical potential as equilibrium criterion",
          "content": "# Unit 4 \u00b7 Lesson 2: Chemical potential as equilibrium criterion\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain chemical potential as equilibrium criterion in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nMatter redistributes between phases until the chemical potential of each transferable species is equal across coexisting phases.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** At equilibrium \u03bc_i^\u03b1=\u03bc_i^\u03b2; \u03bc_i is partial molar Gibbs energy.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nLiquid and vapor can coexist at the same T and P despite sharply different densities because equality of potential, not equality of density, governs equilibrium.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA flash drum contains vapor and liquid at common T and P. Explain why equality of chemical potential for each transferable component is the equilibrium test.\n\nThe phase compositions typically differ; equal \u03bc values are compatible with unequal concentrations because the phase standards differ.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nEqual total Gibbs energies across phases is not the appropriate condition; matching the individual species chemical potentials is essential.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nInterpret multicomponent equilibrium in flash equipment.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for chemical potential as equilibrium criterion.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Chemical potential as equilibrium criterion** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between chemical potential as equilibrium criterion and the application \u201cInterpret multicomponent equilibrium in flash equipment.\u201d The governing relation is **At equilibrium \u03bc_i^\u03b1=\u03bc_i^\u03b2; \u03bc_i is partial molar Gibbs energy.**; the stated failure mode is **Equal total Gibbs energies across phases is not the appropriate condition; matching the individual species chemical potentials is essential.**"
