          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Theory: Thermodynamic Properties of Real Fluids, Departures and Fugacity",
          "content": "## Why this unit matters\n\nBubble, dew, activity coefficients and flash separation. The preceding prerequisite is **Phase equilibrium**; the destination is **Other equilibria**. Engineering students should be able to move from a physical scenario to a transparent mathematical model and back to an interpretation of what would be observed in equipment. Thermodynamic design becomes unreliable when an input is precise to many decimal places while the phase model, standard states or material identities remain unspecified.\n\n## Four connected lessons\n\n### 1. Raoult-law bubble pressure\n\nFor an ideal liquid solution at modest pressure, vapor partial pressures scale with liquid mole fractions and pure-component saturation pressures.\n\n**Useful calculation:** P_bubble=\u03a3 x_i P_i^sat; y_i=x_i P_i^sat/P.\n\n**Physical interpretation:** A richer light-component liquid typically has a higher bubble pressure at fixed temperature when its pure saturation pressure is greater.\n\n**Failure to watch:** An ideal mixture approximation may fail for hydrogen-bonding or strongly dissimilar liquids; vapor nonideality matters at high pressure.\n\n**Decision:** Estimate vaporization onset for binary solvent handling.\n\n### 2. Dew-pressure and dew-temperature calculations\n\nThe first droplet formed from vapor must satisfy vapor and incipient-liquid compositions simultaneously.\n\n**Useful calculation:** At ideal low pressure \u03a3 y_i P/P_i^sat(T)=1 at dew temperature.\n\n**Physical interpretation:** A condenser can be screened for its first-liquid appearance by solving a nonlinear scalar temperature equation.\n\n**Failure to watch:** A dew calculation performed with feed liquid fractions instead of vapor fractions solves a different problem and produces false results.\n\n**Decision:** Set condenser approach temperatures with uncertainty margins.\n\n### 3. Nonideal liquid activities\n\nActivity coefficients correct chemical potential in a liquid mixture when interactions depart from ideal mixing.\n\n**Useful calculation:** Modified Raoult relation y_i P\u2248x_i \u03b3_i P_i^sat under low-pressure vapor conditions.\n\n**Physical interpretation:** A nonideal mixture may exhibit an azeotrope where vapor and liquid compositions become equal at a particular equilibrium state.\n\n**Failure to watch:** Using \u03b3_i=1 without evidence can conceal azeotropes and cause impossible separation specifications.\n\n**Decision:** Use experimental VLE data to decide whether simple correlations are appropriate.\n\n### 4. Flash vaporization and Rachford\u2013Rice\n\nA constant-T,P flash solves component mass balances and equilibrium partitioning together with unknown vapor fraction.\n\n**Useful calculation:** F(\u03b2)=\u03a3 z_i(K_i\u22121)/(1+\u03b2(K_i\u22121))=0, with 0\u2264\u03b2\u22641 and denominator positive.\n\n**Physical interpretation:** For known equilibrium ratios a feasible two-phase solution produces consistent liquid and vapor compositions and an overall component balance.\n\n**Failure to watch:** Blindly solving the scalar equation without a phase-feasibility check may return a nonphysical vapor fraction.\n\n**Decision:** Perform process-level flash checks and sensitivity analysis.\n\n## Unit assessment\n\n- **Problem 1A \u2014 Raoult-law bubble pressure.** Given an engineering case of your choice, identify a defensible thermodynamic boundary and relevant equation, and justify its variables.\n- **Problem 1B \u2014 Data reliability.** Describe a measurement or property-data uncertainty that affects raoult-law bubble pressure and a way to quantify its consequence.\n- **Problem 1C \u2014 Counterexample.** Construct an operating case where the assumption \u201cAn ideal mixture approximation may fail for hydrogen-bonding or strongly dissimilar liquids; vapor nonideality matters at high pressure.\u201d becomes important and would invalidate a simplistic answer.\n- **Problem 2A \u2014 Dew-pressure and dew-temperature calculations.** Given an engineering case of your choice, identify a defensible thermodynamic boundary and relevant equation, and justify its variables.\n- **Problem 2B \u2014 Data reliability.** Describe a measurement or property-data uncertainty that affects dew-pressure and dew-temperature calculations and a way to quantify its consequence.\n- **Problem 2C \u2014 Counterexample.** Construct an operating case where the assumption \u201cA dew calculation performed with feed liquid fractions instead of vapor fractions solves a different problem and produces false results.\u201d becomes important and would invalidate a simplistic answer.\n- **Problem 3A \u2014 Nonideal liquid activities.** Given an engineering case of your choice, identify a defensible thermodynamic boundary and relevant equation, and justify its variables.\n- **Problem 3B \u2014 Data reliability.** Describe a measurement or property-data uncertainty that affects nonideal liquid activities and a way to quantify its consequence.\n- **Problem 3C \u2014 Counterexample.** Construct an operating case where the assumption \u201cUsing \u03b3_i=1 without evidence can conceal azeotropes and cause impossible separation specifications.\u201d becomes important and would invalidate a simplistic answer.\n- **Problem 4A \u2014 Flash vaporization and Rachford\u2013Rice.** Given an engineering case of your choice, identify a defensible thermodynamic boundary and relevant equation, and justify its variables.\n- **Problem 4B \u2014 Data reliability.** Describe a measurement or property-data uncertainty that affects flash vaporization and rachford\u2013rice and a way to quantify its consequence.\n- **Problem 4C \u2014 Counterexample.** Construct an operating case where the assumption \u201cBlindly solving the scalar equation without a phase-feasibility check may return a nonphysical vapor fraction.\u201d becomes important and would invalidate a simplistic answer.\n\n## Assessment evidence"
