          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 2: Dew-pressure and dew-temperature calculations",
          "content": "# Unit 5 \u00b7 Lesson 2: Dew-pressure and dew-temperature calculations\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain dew-pressure and dew-temperature calculations in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nThe first droplet formed from vapor must satisfy vapor and incipient-liquid compositions simultaneously.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** At ideal low pressure \u03a3 y_i P/P_i^sat(T)=1 at dew temperature.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nA condenser can be screened for its first-liquid appearance by solving a nonlinear scalar temperature equation.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA vapor contains 65 mol% of the more volatile species; use illustrative Clausius vapor-pressure curves to find its first condensation temperature at fixed pressure.\n\nDew calculations begin with y rather than x; a robust root solver requires a temperature bracket with a change of sign.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nA dew calculation performed with feed liquid fractions instead of vapor fractions solves a different problem and produces false results.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nSet condenser approach temperatures with uncertainty margins.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for dew-pressure and dew-temperature calculations.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Dew-pressure and dew-temperature calculations** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between dew-pressure and dew-temperature calculations and the application \u201cSet condenser approach temperatures with uncertainty margins.\u201d The governing relation is **At ideal low pressure \u03a3 y_i P/P_i^sat(T)=1 at dew temperature.**; the stated failure mode is **A dew calculation performed with feed liquid fractions instead of vapor fractions solves a different problem and produces false results.**"
