          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 3: Nonideal liquid activities",
          "content": "# Unit 5 \u00b7 Lesson 3: Nonideal liquid activities\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain nonideal liquid activities in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nActivity coefficients correct chemical potential in a liquid mixture when interactions depart from ideal mixing.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** Modified Raoult relation y_i P\u2248x_i \u03b3_i P_i^sat under low-pressure vapor conditions.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nA nonideal mixture may exhibit an azeotrope where vapor and liquid compositions become equal at a particular equilibrium state.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA solvent\u2013water mixture forms a nonideal azeotrope. Compare \u03b3_i=1 with activity-coefficient predictions and assess whether distillation alone can reach the requested purity.\n\nA strongly nonideal mixture can invalidate ordinary relative-volatility assumptions and motivate alternate separation technologies.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nUsing \u03b3_i=1 without evidence can conceal azeotropes and cause impossible separation specifications.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nUse experimental VLE data to decide whether simple correlations are appropriate.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for nonideal liquid activities.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Nonideal liquid activities** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between nonideal liquid activities and the application \u201cUse experimental VLE data to decide whether simple correlations are appropriate.\u201d The governing relation is **Modified Raoult relation y_i P\u2248x_i \u03b3_i P_i^sat under low-pressure vapor conditions.**; the stated failure mode is **Using \u03b3_i=1 without evidence can conceal azeotropes and cause impossible separation specifications.**"
