          "heading": "Experimental Laboratory Investigation: Laboratory 9: Two-phase steam quality",
          "content": "# Laboratory 9: Two-phase steam quality\n\n**Model:** h=hf+x hfg\n\n**Controllable variables:** Mass vapor quality (fraction) and Latent enthalpy (kJ kg\u207b\u00b9).\n\n## Governing relation\n\nh=hf+x hfg. This relation is used for an educational screening calculation, not certified physical-property or process-safety design. Every output must be checked against dimensional consistency, equilibrium assumptions and an independent reference when applied to real chemicals.\n\n## Stated limitations\n\nReference saturated-liquid h_f=600 kJ/kg; both enthalpies at one saturation pressure.\n\n## Step-by-step investigation\n\n1. Record the default inputs and the readout with its units. Sketch the qualitative expected trend before inspecting the chart.\n2. Decrease **Mass vapor quality** to its minimum while keeping **Latent enthalpy** fixed. Record the change and explain which term in the governing equation is responsible.\n3. Increase **Mass vapor quality** to its maximum and compare the response with the first observation. Determine whether the dependence is linear, logarithmic, exponential or nonlinear for a reason you can explain.\n4. Reset, then alter **Latent enthalpy** and repeat. Estimate a finite-difference sensitivity between two nearby parameter values, including its units.\n5. Export the response curve to CSV. Explain how a plotted sensitivity helps select a range for a laboratory measurement or a process-control test.\n6. Identify the first physical mechanism omitted by the model that would become important if the system were pressurized, heated, mixed or scaled up substantially.\n\n## Questions\n\n**Reasoning:** Why is quality not a volume fraction?\n\n**Extension:** Compare 50% quality with the midpoint of vapor and liquid specific volumes.\n\n**Uncertainty:** How would uncertainty in latent enthalpy influence your interpretation of the response curve? Which single additional measurement would best distinguish model error from input uncertainty?\n\n## Evidence to submit\n\nExport a parameter sweep from the interactive model and attach it with your explanation of **h=hf+x hfg**. Demonstrate the predicted change in **Mass vapor quality** at two different settings of **Latent enthalpy**. Conclude by explaining whether the laboratory's approximation **(Reference saturated-liquid h_f=600 kJ/kg; both enthalpies at one saturation pressure.)** would remain credible in a real chemical process."
