          "id": "sec-6-5",
          "secNumber": "6.5",
          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 4: Osmosis and electrolyte solution limits",
          "content": "# Unit 6 \u00b7 Lesson 4: Osmosis and electrolyte solution limits\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain osmosis and electrolyte solution limits in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nSolute activities control solvent chemical potential; dilute ideal solutions exhibit osmotic pressure roughly proportional to total dissolved particle concentration.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** \u03c0\u2248i c R T in an ideal dilute model; i approximates the number of dissolved particles per solute formula unit.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nReverse osmosis requires pressure exceeding the membrane-separated osmotic pressure difference, with further losses and concentration polarization.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA dilute nonelectrolyte concentration is 100 mol/m\u00b3 at 300 K. Ideal osmotic pressure is approximately 249 kPa when i=1.\n\nReal seawater is concentrated and ionic; this dilute estimate is a demonstration of direction and scale, not a membrane-design tool.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nReal electrolytes require activity corrections; ideal van\u2019t Hoff predictions lose reliability at high ionic strength.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nEvaluate membrane driving-force directions with unit-consistent estimates.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for osmosis and electrolyte solution limits.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Osmosis and electrolyte solution limits** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between osmosis and electrolyte solution limits and the application \u201cEvaluate membrane driving-force directions with unit-consistent estimates.\u201d The governing relation is **\u03c0\u2248i c R T in an ideal dilute model; i approximates the number of dissolved particles per solute formula unit.**; the stated failure mode is **Real electrolytes require activity corrections; ideal van\u2019t Hoff predictions lose reliability at high ionic strength.**"
