          "secNumber": "6.7",
          "heading": "Experimental Laboratory Investigation: Laboratory 12: Margules nonideal bubble model",
          "content": "# Laboratory 12: Margules nonideal bubble model\n\n**Model:** P=\u03a3 xi \u03b3i Psati; ln\u03b31=A(1\u2212x)\u00b2\n\n**Controllable variables:** Liquid mole fraction A (fraction) and Margules interaction A (dimensionless).\n\n## Governing relation\n\nP=\u03a3 xi \u03b3i Psati; ln\u03b31=A(1\u2212x)\u00b2. This relation is used for an educational screening calculation, not certified physical-property or process-safety design. Every output must be checked against dimensional consistency, equilibrium assumptions and an independent reference when applied to real chemicals.\n\n## Stated limitations\n\nOne-parameter two-suffix Margules, fixed T=350 K and low-pressure vapor.\n\n## Step-by-step investigation\n\n1. Record the default inputs and the readout with its units. Sketch the qualitative expected trend before inspecting the chart.\n2. Decrease **Liquid mole fraction A** to its minimum while keeping **Margules interaction A** fixed. Record the change and explain which term in the governing equation is responsible.\n3. Increase **Liquid mole fraction A** to its maximum and compare the response with the first observation. Determine whether the dependence is linear, logarithmic, exponential or nonlinear for a reason you can explain.\n4. Reset, then alter **Margules interaction A** and repeat. Estimate a finite-difference sensitivity between two nearby parameter values, including its units.\n5. Export the response curve to CSV. Explain how a plotted sensitivity helps select a range for a laboratory measurement or a process-control test.\n6. Identify the first physical mechanism omitted by the model that would become important if the system were pressurized, heated, mixed or scaled up substantially.\n\n## Questions\n\n**Reasoning:** When can activity corrections create extrema in bubble pressure?\n\n**Extension:** Set A=0 and verify recovery of Raoult-law results.\n\n**Uncertainty:** How would uncertainty in margules interaction a influence your interpretation of the response curve? Which single additional measurement would best distinguish model error from input uncertainty?\n\n## Evidence to submit\n\nExport a parameter sweep from the interactive model and attach it with your explanation of **P=\u03a3 xi \u03b3i Psati; ln\u03b31=A(1\u2212x)\u00b2**. Demonstrate the predicted change in **Liquid mole fraction A** at two different settings of **Margules interaction A**. Conclude by explaining whether the laboratory's approximation **(One-parameter two-suffix Margules, fixed T=350 K and low-pressure vapor.)** would remain credible in a real chemical process."
