          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 2: Temperature effects and van\u2019t Hoff relation",
          "content": "# Unit 7 \u00b7 Lesson 2: Temperature effects and van\u2019t Hoff relation\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain temperature effects and van\u2019t hoff relation in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nEquilibrium constants shift with temperature according to the standard reaction enthalpy, under the usual assumptions for its integration.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** d(ln K)/dT=\u0394_rH\u00b0/(RT\u00b2); for constant \u0394_rH\u00b0, ln(K2/K1)=\u2212\u0394_rH\u00b0/R(1/T2\u22121/T1).\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nAn exothermic reaction generally has smaller K at higher temperature; kinetics may accelerate simultaneously, creating a tradeoff.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA reaction has K=4 at 300 K and \u0394rH\u00b0=\u221240 kJ/mol. Estimate K at 320 K under constant reaction enthalpy.\n\nFor an exothermic reaction K decreases with temperature; this does not automatically imply slower reaction kinetics.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nDo not infer economic reactor performance from K alone: rate, pressure drop, catalyst deactivation and heat removal matter.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nCompare operating-temperature targets with conversion limits.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for temperature effects and van\u2019t hoff relation.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Temperature effects and van\u2019t Hoff relation** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between temperature effects and van\u2019t hoff relation and the application \u201cCompare operating-temperature targets with conversion limits.\u201d The governing relation is **d(ln K)/dT=\u0394_rH\u00b0/(RT\u00b2); for constant \u0394_rH\u00b0, ln(K2/K1)=\u2212\u0394_rH\u00b0/R(1/T2\u22121/T1).**; the stated failure mode is **Do not infer economic reactor performance from K alone: rate, pressure drop, catalyst deactivation and heat removal matter.**"
