          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 3: Reaction extent and equilibrium conversion",
          "content": "# Unit 7 \u00b7 Lesson 3: Reaction extent and equilibrium conversion\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain reaction extent and equilibrium conversion in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nStoichiometric extent tracks every species consistently while a nonlinear equilibrium constraint determines feasible composition.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** n_i=n_i0+\u03bd_i \u03be, together with Q(\u03be,T,P)=K(T).\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nA feed containing excess reactant may shift an equilibrium reaction but changes separation and recycle requirements downstream.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nFor A\u2194B, start with 2 mol A and 0.2 mol B. The allowable forward extent is bounded by availability of A.\n\nSolve only within physical nonnegative species inventories; roots outside that interval describe nonexistent compositions.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nThe allowable extent interval comes from nonnegative mole numbers; solving outside this interval is not a valid chemical state.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nBuild a safeguarded scalar equilibrium solver.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for reaction extent and equilibrium conversion.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Reaction extent and equilibrium conversion** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between reaction extent and equilibrium conversion and the application \u201cBuild a safeguarded scalar equilibrium solver.\u201d The governing relation is **n_i=n_i0+\u03bd_i \u03be, together with Q(\u03be,T,P)=K(T).**; the stated failure mode is **The allowable extent interval comes from nonnegative mole numbers; solving outside this interval is not a valid chemical state.**"
