          "id": "sec-8-2",
          "secNumber": "8.2",
          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 1: Compression and expansion efficiency",
          "content": "# Unit 8 \u00b7 Lesson 1: Compression and expansion efficiency\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain compression and expansion efficiency in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nAdiabatic machines are benchmarked against reversible isentropic endpoints; real performance is defined by enthalpy changes.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** For an ideal gas with constant k, T2s/T1=(P2/P1)^((k\u22121)/k); compressor \u03b7_is=(h2s\u2212h1)/(h2\u2212h1).\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nA compressor with reduced efficiency delivers hotter discharge and consumes more shaft power for the same inlet and outlet pressures.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nAir enters an ideal-gas compressor at 300 K and experiences P2/P1=4 with k=1.4. Estimate isentropic discharge temperature and compare \u03b7_is=0.78.\n\nActual compressor discharge temperature exceeds the isentropic estimate when \u03b7_is is below one; the model ignores variable heat capacities.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nDo not use ideal-gas k at supercritical conditions or near condensation; multi-stage compression may require intercooling and a real-fluid EOS.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nCalculate heat rejection needs from efficiency trends.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for compression and expansion efficiency.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Compression and expansion efficiency** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between compression and expansion efficiency and the application \u201cCalculate heat rejection needs from efficiency trends.\u201d The governing relation is **For an ideal gas with constant k, T2s/T1=(P2/P1)^((k\u22121)/k); compressor \u03b7_is=(h2s\u2212h1)/(h2\u2212h1).**; the stated failure mode is **Do not use ideal-gas k at supercritical conditions or near condensation; multi-stage compression may require intercooling and a real-fluid EOS.**"
