          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 2: Refrigeration and heat pumps",
          "content": "# Unit 8 \u00b7 Lesson 2: Refrigeration and heat pumps\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain refrigeration and heat pumps in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nA vapor-compression cycle transfers heat from low to high temperature by consuming work and exploiting phase changes.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** COP_refrigerator=Q_c/W; COP_heatpump=Q_h/W; energy conservation gives Q_h=Q_c+W.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nRefrigerant approach temperatures and compressor inefficiency strongly influence practical electricity consumption.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nA refrigerator removes 3.5 kW from cold storage while consuming 1.0 kW electrical-equivalent shaft input. Its COP is 3.5 and it rejects 4.5 kW.\n\nThe electric meter and compressor shaft can differ due to drive losses; COP boundaries must be aligned before comparing systems.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nAn ideal Carnot COP is an upper bound and not a forecast of real equipment performance; refrigerant environmental and safety constraints also matter.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nAnalyze seasonal sensitivity without inventing vendor performance data.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for refrigeration and heat pumps.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Refrigeration and heat pumps** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between refrigeration and heat pumps and the application \u201cAnalyze seasonal sensitivity without inventing vendor performance data.\u201d The governing relation is **COP_refrigerator=Q_c/W; COP_heatpump=Q_h/W; energy conservation gives Q_h=Q_c+W.**; the stated failure mode is **An ideal Carnot COP is an upper bound and not a forecast of real equipment performance; refrigerant environmental and safety constraints also matter.**"
