          "heading": "\u00b7 Lesson 3: Thermodynamics of separation work",
          "content": "# Unit 8 \u00b7 Lesson 3: Thermodynamics of separation work\n\n> **Learning objectives.** Explain thermodynamics of separation work in physical terms; identify the relevant state or balance variables; apply a quantitative relation only within its validity range; and critically review a proposed process-engineering decision.\n\n## The physical question\n\nMixing creates entropy, and unmixing requires energy or available work even before real equipment losses are included.\n\n## Governing relation and interpretation\n\n**Key relation:** For ideal mixing \u0394G_mix=nRT \u03a3 x_i ln x_i; minimum reversible separation work is \u2212\u0394G_mix for an appropriate specified process.\n\n## Engineering example and model boundary\n\nVery dilute solutes may demand substantial work per mole recovered because an extreme purity specification is thermodynamically demanding.\n\n## Worked investigation\n\nAt 300 K, separate an equimolar ideal liquid mixture containing 100 mol total into pure components. The ideal mixing Gibbs energy has magnitude nRT ln2.\n\nThe minimum reversible separation work is positive because spontaneous mixing lowers Gibbs energy; real separation heat demand is larger.\n\n## Where the relation breaks down\n\nThe minimum reversible work is not the steam or electric use of a real column, which includes finite heat transfer and imperfect separation.\n\n## Engineering decision and data checklist\n\nExplain why recovery and purity targets increase cost.\n\n## Guided problems\n\n1. **Definition and boundary.** Describe the relevant system and identify which properties must be uniform before using the governing relation for thermodynamics of separation work.\n2. **Derivation.** State which conservation principle, potential differential or equilibrium condition supports the key relation. Explain each approximation that is needed to reach the displayed form.\n3. **Units and sign.** Determine the natural units of each term. Describe a sign error or reference-state error that would produce a misleading yet numerical-looking result.\n4. **Sensitivity.** Change one physically adjustable input by approximately 10% and predict the direction of the response before performing an exact calculation.\n5. **Independent validation.** Propose a laboratory measurement or alternate property relation that would help detect a model error.\n6. **Engineering judgment.** Explain which additional information would be mandatory before the calculation could support purchase, construction or operation of real equipment.\n\n## Mini-case: two competing models\n\nSuppose a project team has a quick spreadsheet estimate and a detailed simulation package that disagree. Model A is simpler and transparent; Model B includes correlations for effects missing from Model A. First, compare both results under the same physical conditions and reference convention. Then determine whether they are truly independent or share the same underlying property parameters. A difference between them is not automatically proof that Model B is better. Ask which result fits experimental observations, and whether the disagreement is material to the stated decision. Apply this comparison specifically to **Thermodynamics of separation work** using the assumptions discussed in this lesson.\n\n## Lesson synthesis\n\n**Subject focus:** The key distinguishing result of this lesson is the link between thermodynamics of separation work and the application \u201cExplain why recovery and purity targets increase cost.\u201d The governing relation is **For ideal mixing \u0394G_mix=nRT \u03a3 x_i ln x_i; minimum reversible separation work is \u2212\u0394G_mix for an appropriate specified process.**; the stated failure mode is **The minimum reversible work is not the steam or electric use of a real column, which includes finite heat transfer and imperfect separation.**"
