          "heading": "Experimental Laboratory Investigation: Laboratory 16: Equilibrium liquid\u2013liquid extraction",
          "content": "# Laboratory 16: Equilibrium liquid\u2013liquid extraction\n\n**Model:** Recovered fraction=K Vorg/(Vaq+K Vorg)\n\n**Controllable variables:** Partition ratio K (dimensionless) and Organic solvent volume (L).\n\n## Governing relation\n\nRecovered fraction=K Vorg/(Vaq+K Vorg). This relation is used for an educational screening calculation, not certified physical-property or process-safety design. Every output must be checked against dimensional consistency, equilibrium assumptions and an independent reference when applied to real chemicals.\n\n## Stated limitations\n\nConstant dilute partition ratio; aqueous volume fixed at 8 L.\n\n## Step-by-step investigation\n\n1. Record the default inputs and the readout with its units. Sketch the qualitative expected trend before inspecting the chart.\n2. Decrease **Partition ratio K** to its minimum while keeping **Organic solvent volume** fixed. Record the change and explain which term in the governing equation is responsible.\n3. Increase **Partition ratio K** to its maximum and compare the response with the first observation. Determine whether the dependence is linear, logarithmic, exponential or nonlinear for a reason you can explain.\n4. Reset, then alter **Organic solvent volume** and repeat. Estimate a finite-difference sensitivity between two nearby parameter values, including its units.\n5. Export the response curve to CSV. Explain how a plotted sensitivity helps select a range for a laboratory measurement or a process-control test.\n6. Identify the first physical mechanism omitted by the model that would become important if the system were pressurized, heated, mixed or scaled up substantially.\n\n## Questions\n\n**Reasoning:** Why does more solvent provide diminishing gains in recovered fraction?\n\n**Extension:** Find a solvent volume that yields at least 75% extraction.\n\n**Uncertainty:** How would uncertainty in organic solvent volume influence your interpretation of the response curve? Which single additional measurement would best distinguish model error from input uncertainty?\n\n## Evidence to submit\n\nExport a parameter sweep from the interactive model and attach it with your explanation of **Recovered fraction=K Vorg/(Vaq+K Vorg)**. Demonstrate the predicted change in **Partition ratio K** at two different settings of **Organic solvent volume**. Conclude by explaining whether the laboratory's approximation **(Constant dilute partition ratio; aqueous volume fixed at 8 L.)** would remain credible in a real chemical process."
